## More Than Two: elif

When you have multiple conditions to check, chain them with `elif` (short for "else if"):

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let score = 85;

  if score >= 90 {
    io.println("Grade: A");
  } elif score >= 80 {
    io.println("Grade: B");
  } elif score >= 70 {
    io.println("Grade: C");
  } elif score >= 60 {
    io.println("Grade: D");
  } else {
    io.println("Grade: F");
  }

  return 0;
}
```

### How elif Works

XIOM checks conditions from top to bottom. The FIRST matching condition wins:

```xiom
let number = 15;

if number > 20 {
  io.println("Big");
} elif number > 10 {
  io.println("Medium");  // This runs! (15 > 10)
} elif number > 0 {
  io.println("Small");   // Skipped — already matched above
} else {
  io.println("Zero or negative");
}
```

### elif vs Multiple ifs

```xiom
// Multiple ifs — ALL are checked independently
if score >= 90 { io.println("A"); }
if score >= 80 { io.println("B"); }  // Also runs for score=95! BAD!

// if/elif/else — only FIRST match runs
if score >= 90 { io.println("A"); }
elif score >= 80 { io.println("B"); }  // Only for 80-89. GOOD!
```

### Order Matters

Always check from most specific to least specific:

```xiom
// WRONG order — "A" never runs because >= 80 catches it first!
if score >= 80 { io.println("B"); }
elif score >= 90 { io.println("A"); }

// CORRECT order — most specific first
if score >= 90 { io.println("A"); }
elif score >= 80 { io.println("B"); }
```

### Structure

```xiom
if condition1 {
  // first match
} elif condition2 {
  // second match
} elif condition3 {
  // third match
} else {
  // none matched
}
```

You can have as many `elif` branches as you need. The final `else` is optional.

